ighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Andy Ogles (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Tennessee's 5th District is an open seat after incumbent Andy Ogles lost renomination, leaving Republican Charlie Hatcher to face Democrat Chaz Molder. The district leans Republican by about ten points, and the political environment this year favors Republicans. The only public poll, conducted for Democratic interests in late August, showed Hatcher ahead by eight points.\n\nMolder has outraised Hatcher substantially and holds a large cash advantage, and the primary upset removed Ogles's controversies from the ballot. Still, expert raters overwhelmingly place the race in the Safe or Solid Republican column, and prediction markets give Molder only a small chance. The race appears likely to stay in Republican hands, with the main question being the final margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.079, "margin": -8.8}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0675, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0985, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0593, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0597, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0699, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0949, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0595, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0915, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0612, "margin": -10.0}]}